    "thm-cg-sortable-meet-join-closure-and-cambrian-quotient": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-cg-recursive-sortable-projection-and-cambrian-congruence",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(1) Sortable equivalence and quotient order.** Define the **sortable equivalence** $\\sim_c$ on $W$ by\n\n$$x\\sim_c y:\\iff \\pi_c(x)=\\pi_c(y),$$\n\nwrite $[x]_c:=\\{y\\in W:\\pi_c(y)=\\pi_c(x)\\}$ for the $\\sim_c$-class of $x$ and $W/{\\sim_c}:=\\{[x]_c:x\\in W\\}$, and let $p_c\\colon W\\to W/{\\sim_c}$, $p_c(x):=[x]_c$, be the quotient map. The **sortable quotient order** on $W/{\\sim_c}$ is\n\n$$[x]_c\\le_c[y]_c:\\iff \\pi_c(x)\\le_R\\pi_c(y).$$\n\nThis is independent of the chosen representatives because each class has a single $\\pi_c$-image; it is the order induced by $\\le_R$ on the image of $\\pi_c$.\n\n**(2) Proposed quotient operations.** On classes define\n\n$$[x]_c\\vee[y]_c:=[x\\vee y]_c,\\qquad [x]_c\\wedge[y]_c:=[x\\wedge y]_c,$$\n\nthe **proposed quotient operations** of [[def-cg-finite-lattice-congruence-and-interval-projections]] (1) specialized to the weak-order lattice $W$.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-cg-initial-letter-sortable-projection",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For an initial letter $s$ of $c$, the recursion is $\\pi_c(w)=s\\pi_{scs}(sw)$ when $w\\ge_Rs$ and $\\pi_c(w)=\\pi_{c\\prime}(w_J)$ when $w\\not\\ge_Rs$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.4",
            "3.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-cg-coxeter-oriented-euler-form-and-c-sorting-word",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(3) The periodic word and admissible position sets.** Fix a reduced Coxeter word $s_1\\cdots s_n$ for $c$ and form the half-infinite periodic word\n$$c^\\infty:=s_1\\cdots s_n\\,|\\,s_1\\cdots s_n\\,|\\,\\cdots,$$\nwhere the symbols $|$ are inert dividers after every block of $n$ letters and are ignored when subwords are evaluated. A **position set** for $c^\\infty$ is a finite strictly increasing sequence of positions $i_1<\\cdots<i_k$; its **value** is $s_{i_1}\\cdots s_{i_k}\\in W$, and it is **admissible for $w\\in W$** when its value is $w$ and $k=\\ell(w)$, equivalently when its letters form a reduced expression of $w$. The $c^\\infty$**-sorting word** of $w$ is the lexicographically earliest admissible position set for $w$: least first position, then least second position, and so on. The **block sequence** of an admissible position set is the sequence $T_1,T_2,\\dots$ in which $T_j\\subseteq S$ is the set of letters of the subword occurring between the $(j-1)$-st and the $j$-th divider; it is read up to the last nonempty set.",
          "uses": [
            "2.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-cg-sortable-element-skip-roots-and-cone",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "c-sortability is the weak-decrease-by-inclusion condition on sorting-word blocks, and Cone_c(v) is the intersection of the skip-root halfspaces.",
          "uses": [
            "2.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-cg-sortable-skip-basis-cover-roots-and-chamber-unions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The recursive projection is well-defined, sortable-output, monotone and the greatest c-sortable element below w.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.3",
            "2.5",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-cg-sortable-recursion-output-and-initial-choice-independence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$\\pi_c(w)$ is c-sortable and below $w$, fixes every c-sortable element, detects whether an initial letter lies below its input, and is compatible with parabolic prefixes.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.3",
            "2.4",
            "2.5",
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-cg-uniform-omega-positive-and-aligned-sortability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "A Coxeter element c-sortable element is exactly a c-aligned element.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-cg-greedy-sorting-word-and-rank-two-alignment",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "(ii) $w\\in W$ is $c$**-aligned with respect to $W'$** when either $\\omega_c$ restricts to zero on $\\Phi\\cap P$ and $N(w^{-1})\\cap(\\Phi\\cap P)$ is empty or a singleton, or $\\omega_c(\\beta_{u_1},\\beta_{u_m})>0$ and $N(w^{-1})\\cap(\\Phi\\cap P)$ is empty, the singleton $\\{\\beta_{u_m}\\}$, or an initial segment $\\{\\beta_{u_1},\\dots,\\beta_{u_k}\\}$; and $w$ is $c$**-aligned** when it is $c$-aligned with respect to every noncommutative generalized rank-two parabolic subgroup of $W$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-cg-finite-rank-two-inversion-set-recognition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(1) Recognition.** The following are equivalent:\n\n(i) $I=N(w)$ for some $w\\in W$;\n\n(ii) for every noncommutative generalized rank-two parabolic subgroup $W_P$, the intersection $I\\cap\\Phi_P^+$ is empty, an initial segment, or a final segment.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-cg-geometric-inversion-set",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "$$N(w):=\\{\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+:\\rho(w)\\alpha\\in\\Phi_-\\}=\\Phi_+\\cap\\rho(w)^{-1}\\Phi_-.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "lem-cg-weak-order-is-a-graded-partial-order",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "\nLet $(S,m)$ be a finite Coxeter matrix, $W$ the presented group with length\n$\\ell$, descent sets $D_L,D_R$, weak orders $\\le_R,\\le_L$ and intervals\n$[u,v]_R,[u,v]_L$ as in [[def-cg-left-right-weak-order-and-descents]], with\ninversion sets $N(w)=\\{\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+:\\rho(w)\\alpha\\in\\Phi_-\\}$ and the\nrecursion of [[def-cg-geometric-inversion-set]] (2). Then:\n\n**(1) Partial orders.** $\\le_R$ and $\\le_L$ are partial orders on $W$, both\nwith minimum $1$; $u\\le_R v$ and $\\ell(u)=\\ell(v)$ imply $u=v$; and inversion\n$w\\mapsto w^{-1}$ is an order isomorphism $(W,\\le_R)\\to(W,\\le_L)$, i.e.\n$u\\le_R v\\iff u^{-1}\\le_L v^{-1}$.\n\n**(2) Covers.** For all $u,v\\in W$,\n\n$$u\\lessdot_R v\\iff v=us\\text{ for some }s\\in S\\text{ with }\\ell(v)=\\ell(u)+1,\\qquad u\\lessdot_L v\\iff v=su\\text{ for some }s\\in S\\text{ with }\\ell(v)=\\ell(u)+1.$$\n\nMoreover $u\\le_R v$ if and only if there is a chain\n$u=u_0\\lessdot_R u_1\\lessdot_R\\cdots\\lessdot_R u_m=v$, and then necessarily\n$m=\\ell(v)-\\ell(u)$; the same holds in $\\le_L$.\n\n**(3) Intervals are finite and graded.** For every $k\\ge0$ the ball\n$\\{w\\in W:\\ell(w)\\le k\\}$ is finite, with at most $1+|S|+\\cdots+|S|^k$\nelements. Consequently, whenever $u\\le_R v$, the interval $[u,v]_R$ is finite\nand $x\\mapsto\\ell(x)-\\ell(u)$ is a rank function on it in the sense of\n[[def-graded-poset-and-rank]]; in particular every maximal chain in $[u,v]_R$\nhas exactly $\\ell(v)-\\ell(u)+1$ elements, and if $u^{-1}v=s'_1\\cdots s'_m$ is\na reduced expression then\n$u\\lessdot_R us'_1\\lessdot_R\\cdots\\lessdot_R u s'_1\\cdots s'_m=v$ is such a\nmaximal chain. The same statements hold for $\\le_L$.\n\n**(4) Inversion-set criterion.** For all $u,v\\in W$,\n\n$$u\\le_R v\\iff N(u^{-1})\\subseteq N(v^{-1}),\\qquad u\\le_L v\\iff N(u)\\subseteq N(v);$$\n\nmoreover $|N(w^{-1})|=|N(w)|=\\ell(w)$ for every $w$. Equivalently,\n$w\\mapsto N(w^{-1})$ embeds $(W,\\le_R)$ into the lattice of subsets of\n$\\Phi_+$ as an order-preserving and length-preserving map. The criterion is\nnot the definition of $\\le_R$; it is derived from\n[[def-cg-left-right-weak-order-and-descents]].\n\n**(5) Descents and roots.** For all $w\\in W$ and $s\\in S$,\n\n$$s\\in D_L(w)\\iff e_s\\in N(w^{-1})\\iff\\rho(w^{-1})e_s\\in\\Phi_-,\\qquad s\\in D_R(w)\\iff e_s\\in N(w)\\iff\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_-.$$\n\nNo Choice is used.\n",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3",
            "2.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(1) The root-reflection dictionary.** For $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ choose $w\\in W$, $s\\in S$ with $\\alpha=\\rho(w)e_s$ and put $t_\\alpha:=wsw^{-1}\\in T$. Then:\n(i) $t_\\alpha$ is independent of the chosen representation of $\\alpha$;\n(ii) $\\rho(t_\\alpha)=r_\\alpha$, the reflection with normal $\\alpha$; and $t_{\\rho(w)\\alpha}=w\\,t_\\alpha\\,w^{-1}$ for all $w\\in W$, $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$;\n(iii) $t_{-\\alpha}=t_\\alpha$, and for $\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\Phi$ one has $t_\\alpha=t_\\beta$ if and only if $\\alpha=\\pm\\beta$;\n(iv) the induced map $\\{\\pm\\alpha:\\alpha\\in\\Phi\\}\\to T$ is a bijection; so is the map $\\Phi_+\\to T$, $\\alpha\\mapsto t_\\alpha$.\n\n**(2) Inversion formula.** For every $w\\in W$ one has $|N(w)|=\\ell(w)$ (with $N$ as in [[def-cg-geometric-inversion-set]]); and for every reduced expression $w=s_1\\cdots s_n$,\n$$N(w)=\\bigl\\{\\rho(s_{i+1}\\cdots s_n)^{-1}e_{s_i}:1\\le i\\le n\\bigr\\},\\qquad N(w^{-1})=\\bigl\\{\\rho(s_1\\cdots s_{i-1})e_{s_i}:1\\le i\\le n\\bigr\\},$$\nthe displayed elements being pairwise distinct positive roots.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "2.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "thm-cg-weak-order-meet-semilattice-and-finite-lattice",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(2) Finite Coxeter groups are lattices.** If $W$ is finite, then\n$(W,\\le_R)$ and $(W,\\le_L)$ are lattices with minimum $1$ and maximum $w_0$;\nthat is, every subset of $W$ has a meet and a join, and for the empty subset\n\n$$\\bigwedge\\varnothing=w_0,\\qquad \\bigvee\\varnothing=1.$$\n\nMoreover $w\\le_R w_0$ for every $w\\in W$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F13",
          "source": "lem-cg-weak-parabolic-projection-and-cover-joins",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(3) Meet and join preservation.** For all $x,y\\in W$,\n\n$$(x\\wedge y)_J=x_J\\wedge y_J,\\qquad (x\\vee y)_J=x_J\\vee y_J.$$\n\nThus $w\\mapsto w_J$ is a surjective lattice homomorphism from the finite weak order on $W$ to the induced weak order on $W_J$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "2.4",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "def-cg-left-right-weak-order-and-descents",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "$$u\\le_R v\\iff v=ux\\text{ for some }x\\in W\\text{ with }\\ell(v)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(x),$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F14",
          "source": "def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "each of $\\ell(sw)-\\ell(w)$ and $\\ell(ws)-\\ell(w)$ lies in $\\{\\pm1\\}$\n([[thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness]] (1)).",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F15",
          "source": "def-cg-finite-lattice-congruence-and-interval-projections",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(1) Lattice congruences and projected endpoints.** An equivalence relation $\\theta$ on $L$ is a **lattice congruence** if $x\\equiv_\\theta x'$ and $y\\equiv_\\theta y'$ imply $x\\wedge y\\equiv_\\theta x'\\wedge y'$ and $x\\vee y\\equiv_\\theta x'\\vee y'$. For $x\\in L$ write $[x]_\\theta$ for the **class** of $x$. On classes this defines the **proposed quotient operations** $[x]_\\theta\\vee[y]_\\theta:=[x\\vee y]_\\theta$ and $[x]_\\theta\\wedge[y]_\\theta:=[x\\wedge y]_\\theta$; the **proposed lower endpoint** $\\pi_\\downarrow(x)$ and **upper endpoint** $\\pi_\\uparrow(x)$ of a class are its least and greatest members. The definition asserts neither that the quotient operations are independent of representatives nor that endpoints exist; both are proved in [[lem-cg-lattice-quotient-descent-and-class-intervals]].",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F16",
          "source": "def-cg-finite-reflection-arrangement-and-spherical-chambers",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Since $W$ is finite, $B$ is positive definite\n([[thm-cg-finite-type-positive-definite-criterion]], clause (1),\n[[lem-cg-diagram-products-and-invariant-form-comparison]], clause (4)); hence\n$b:V\\to V^*$, $b(v):=B(v,\\cdot)$, is a linear isomorphism with\n$b(\\rho(w)v)=w\\cdot b(v)$ for all $w\\in W$ and $v\\in V$, because $B$ is $\\rho$-invariant\n([[lem-cg-reflection-representation-descends-and-root-norms]],\n[[def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map]]). **Identify $V$ with $V^*$ by $b$ only\nnow**, and transfer $C$, $C^\\circ$ and the $H_\\alpha$ along $b^{-1}$; with the same letters\n\n$$C=\\{v\\in V:B(v,e_s)\\ge0\\text{ for every }s\\in S\\},\\qquad C^\\circ=\\{v\\in V:B(v,e_s)>0\\text{ for every }s\\in S\\},\\qquad H_\\alpha=\\{v\\in V:B(v,\\alpha)=0\\}.$$\n\nGive $V$ the metric topology of the inner product $B$\n([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]], [[def-metric-space]],\n[[def-metric-topology]]); it is canonical because $B$ is determined by $(W,S)$, and $b$ is an\nisometry for $B$ and its transferred form, so topological notions may be moved across the\nidentification.\n\n**(1) The finite reflection arrangement.** Put\n$$\\mathcal A:=\\{H_\\alpha:\\alpha\\in\\Phi\\}.$$\nEach $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ satisfies $B(\\alpha,\\alpha)=1\\ne0$\n([[lem-cg-reflection-representation-descends-and-root-norms]], clause (3)), so $H_\\alpha$ is\nthe kernel of the nonzero linear functional $B(\\cdot,\\alpha)$ and in particular contains $0$;\n$\\mathcal A$ is finite because $\\Phi$ is the image of the finite set $W\\times S$ under\n$(w,s)\\mapsto\\rho(w)e_s$; and $\\mathcal A$ is permuted by $\\rho(W)$, since\n$\\rho(w)H_\\alpha=H_{\\rho(w)\\alpha}$ for all $w\\in W$, $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ (the form $B$ is\n$\\rho$-invariant). A **chamber** of $\\mathcal A$ is a connected component of\n$V\\setminus\\bigcup_{\\alpha\\in\\Phi}H_\\alpha$, and a **closed chamber** is the closure of a\nchamber ([[def-connected-space]], [[def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent]],\n[[def-metric-topology]]). For $I\\subseteq S$ put\n$$\\overline{C_I}:=\\{v\\in C:B(v,e_s)=0\\text{ for }s\\in I\\},\\qquad C_I:=\\{v\\in C:B(v,e_s)=0\\text{ for }s\\in I\\text{ and }B(v,e_s)>0\\text{ for }s\\notin I\\},$$\nthe **closed face** and the **open face** of $C$ of type $I$; for $w\\in W$ put\n$wC:=\\rho(w)C$, $w\\overline{C_I}:=\\rho(w)\\overline{C_I}$ and $wC_I:=\\rho(w)C_I$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F16",
          "source": "thm-cg-finite-chamber-tiling-and-coset-face-identification",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(1) The chamber tiling.** $U=V^*$, and under the identification of the definition\n$V=\\bigcup_{w\\in W}wC$; moreover\n$$V\\setminus\\bigcup_{\\alpha\\in\\Phi}H_\\alpha=\\bigsqcup_{w\\in W}wC^\\circ,$$\nthe connected components of $V\\setminus\\bigcup_\\alpha H_\\alpha$ are exactly the sets\n$wC^\\circ$ $(w\\in W)$ with closures $\\overline{wC^\\circ}=wC$, so the closed chambers of\n$\\mathcal A$ are exactly the sets $wC$; the map $w\\mapsto wC^\\circ$ is a bijection from $W$\nonto the set of chambers, distinct closed chambers have disjoint interiors, and every\n$W$-orbit in $V$ meets $C$ in exactly one point.",
          "uses": [
            "2.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F17",
          "source": "thm-cg-root-sign-and-simple-reflection-positivity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "**(2) Roots have a sign.** Every root $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ lies in $V_+\\setminus\\{0\\}$ or in $-V_+\\setminus\\{0\\}$, and not in both. Hence, with\n$$\\Phi_+:=\\Phi\\cap V_+,\\qquad \\Phi_-:=\\Phi\\cap(-V_+),$$\none has $\\Phi=\\Phi_+\\sqcup\\Phi_-$, $\\Phi_-=-\\Phi_+$, $e_s\\in\\Phi_+$ for every $s\\in S$, and for every $w\\in W$ and $s\\in S$\n$$\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_+\\iff w^{-1}C^\\circ\\subseteq B_s,\\qquad \\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_-\\iff w^{-1}C^\\circ\\subseteq sB_s.$$\nIn particular $f(\\alpha)>0$ for all $f\\in C^\\circ$ when $\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+$, and $f(\\alpha)<0$ for all $f\\in C^\\circ$ when $\\alpha\\in\\Phi_-$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F18",
          "source": "lem-cg-sortable-cone-criterion-and-projection-monotonicity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The cone criterion is $\\pi_c(w)=v\\iff wC\\subseteq\\operatorname{Cone}_c(v)$ for c-sortable $v$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.5",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F19",
          "source": "lem-cg-sortable-skips-basis-and-cover-decomposition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Negative skip roots are exactly negative cover roots; a sortable $v$ with initial cover $s$ decomposes as $v=s\\vee v_J$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.5",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F20",
          "source": "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "$$R:=\\{s^2:s\\in S\\}\\cup\\{(st)^{m(s,t)}:s,t\\in S,\\ m(s,t)<\\infty\\},$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F21",
          "source": "def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**(1)** Write $\\mathrm{GL}(V)$ for the group of invertible linear maps $V\\to V$, the unit group of the monoid of linear endomorphisms under composition ([[def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map]], [[def-vector-space-of-linear-maps]], [[lem-composition-and-identity-linear-maps]], [[lem-monoid-units-form-a-group]]). The **canonical reflection homomorphism** is the group homomorphism $\\rho:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}(V)$ ([[def-group-homomorphism]]) that satisfies $\\rho(s)=r_s$ for every $s\\in S$; its existence and uniqueness are established by [[lem-cg-reflection-representation-descends-and-root-norms]], which is this definition's recorded justifier. For $w\\in W$ and $v\\in V$ write $\\rho(w)v$ for the image.",
          "uses": [
            "2.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F21",
          "source": "def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "$$r_a(v):=v-\\frac{2B(v,a)}{B(a,a)}a .$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F21",
          "source": "def-cg-finite-reflection-arrangement-and-spherical-chambers",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "$\\mathcal A$ is permuted by $\\rho(W)$, since\n$\\rho(w)H_\\alpha=H_{\\rho(w)\\alpha}$ for all $w\\in W$, $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ (the form $B$ is\n$\\rho$-invariant).",
          "uses": [
            "2.5"
          ]
        }
      ],
